{
  "skillId": "expand-brackets",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
"intro": "**Expanding** brackets means rewriting a product like $a(b+c)$ without brackets, by multiplying every term inside by the term outside. This rule is the **distributive law**.",
"facts": [
          "The distributive law is $a(b+c) = ab + ac$: the outside term multiplies **every** term inside.",
          "Keep the sign in front of each inside term: $a(b-c) = ab - ac$.",
          "A letter multiplied by itself becomes a power: $x \\times x = x^2$.",
          "After expanding, **collect any like terms** to write the answer in simplest form."
        ],
"steps": [
          "Multiply each term.",
          "Keep each sign.",
          "Collect like terms."
        ],
"workedExamples": [
  {
    "question_text": "Expand.\n(a) $4(t+6)$\n(b) $5(2h-3)$",
    "solution_text": "(a) Multiply each term inside by $4$.\n$4(t+6)$\n$=4(t)+4(6)$\n$=4t+24$\n(b) Keep the subtraction sign.\n$5(2h-3)$\n$=5(2h)-5(3)$\n$=10h-15$"
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Expand.\n(a) $-3(2p-5)$\n(b) $-(4q+7)$",
    "solution_text": "(a) Treat the second term as $-5$. A negative times a negative is positive.\n$-3(2p-5)$\n$=(-3)(2p)+(-3)(-5)$\n$=-6p+15$\n(b) The minus in front means multiply by $-1$.\n$-(4q+7)$\n$=(-1)(4q)+(-1)(7)$\n$=-4q-7$"
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Expand $3x(2x-y)$.",
    "solution_text": "Multiply both terms by $3x$.\n$3x(2x-y)$\n$=3x(2x)-3x(y)$\n$=6x^2-3xy$\nThe two terms are unlike, so they cannot be collected."
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Expand and simplify.\n(a) $2(a-3b+4)$\n(b) $3(2z+1)-2(z-4)$",
    "solution_text": "(a) Multiply all three terms by $2$.\n$2(a-3b+4)$\n$=2(a)-2(3b)+2(4)$\n$=2a-6b+8$\n(b) Expand each bracket, including the negative multiplier on the second bracket.\n$3(2z+1)-2(z-4)$\n$=6z+3-2z+8$\n$=4z+11$"
  }
]
},
